Handle these as two editor/UX problems first. Do not change the raycaster
mathematics speculatively.

==================================================
1. HALF WALLS — VERIFY PAINT STATE BEFORE RUNTIME
==================================================

The static runtime chain is already clean:

    halfCells[cell]
        ->
    HC$ 196-character string
        ->
    HC$(MI2:MI2)
        ->
    WH=INT(WH/2)
        ->
    SY1/SY2

Do NOT rewrite HC$, MI2, WH or projection yet.

The leading explanation is:

    HALF is captured when the map cell is painted.

Selecting HALF afterward does not retroactively alter an existing wall.

Prove this in the editor first.

Add a temporary/debug readout for the cell under the cursor or selected cell:

    CELL index
    map value
    halfCells[index]

Example:

    CELL 37
    WALL 2
    HALF 0

Then:

1. place a wall while FULL is selected
2. select HALF without clicking wall again
3. confirm HALF remains 0
4. click the existing wall with HALF selected
5. confirm HALF becomes 1
6. generate BAS and confirm the corresponding HC$ character is 1

If this sequence behaves as above, the renderer is innocent.

==================================================
2. MAKE SHAPE APPLY TO EXISTING BLOCK WALLS
==================================================

Yes: clicking an existing BLOCK wall should be able to modify its FULL/HALF
state.

The current UX should behave as "place or modify", not "shape only captured
on first placement".

When BLOCK mode is active and a wall cell is clicked:

    existing or new wall
        ->
    set halfCells[index] = brushHalf

Do not require the user to erase and repaint the wall to change its height.

If the click also paints a selected wall material/type, preserve the existing
normal painting behaviour.

But the important invariant is:

    every successful BLOCK-wall click updates halfCells for that cell.

==================================================
3. CLEAR HALF METADATA WHEN CELL STOPS BEING A BLOCK
==================================================

When a map cell is changed to FLOOR or another non-block object, clear stale
block-height metadata:

    halfCells[index] = false

Otherwise:

    wall HALF
    -> erase to floor
    -> later place another wall

could silently resurrect the previous HALF setting.

Likewise RESET / CLEAR MAP should reset halfCells appropriately.

Save/load should continue carrying halfCells.

==================================================
4. ADD A VISIBLE HALF-WALL MAP AFFORDANCE
==================================================

The map editor should visibly distinguish HALF from FULL blocks.

For example:

    FULL wall = normal filled tile
    HALF wall = same tile colour with a small H marker / inner border /
                horizontal split indicator

Do not rely only on the FULL/HALF toolbar button.

The user needs to be able to look at the map and see which existing cells have
the flag.

This also makes the paint-time bug immediately obvious if it ever recurs.

==================================================
5. DO NOT CHANGE HC$ YET
==================================================

The BASIC substring expression:

    HC$(MI2:MI2)

uses the same substring model already used successfully by:

    M$(MI:MI)

and both strings are 196 characters.

There is currently no evidence that HC$ itself is the problem.

Only investigate hardware substring semantics if:

    editor says HALF=1
    generated HC$ has 1 at correct index
    final BAS contains the correct test
    BUT hardware still draws full height.

If that happens, make a tiny isolated EZ-BASIC HC$ test rather than changing
the raycaster.

==================================================
6. THIN WALL ERASE — ADD EXPLICIT ERASE MODE
==================================================

Add an explicit:

    ERASE

brush/mode.

It should be visually obvious and live alongside BLOCK / THIN / decoration
editing.

ERASE must operate according to what the cursor actually targets.

If cursor is inside a cell:

    clear map cell to FLOOR
    clear halfCells[index]
    clear decoration/object occupying that cell if appropriate

If cursor is near an H/V edge:

    clear that exact hEdges/vEdges entry

Do NOT use toggle semantics while ERASE is active.

ERASE means:

    set to empty

never:

    empty -> create something

==================================================
7. DO NOT MAKE FLOOR DELETE ALL FOUR EDGES
==================================================

Do NOT automatically erase every edge surrounding a cell whenever FLOOR is
painted.

Thin walls live on shared boundaries.

For example:

    cell A | thin wall | cell B

Painting FLOOR into cell A should not necessarily destroy a wall that the user
intended as the boundary of cell B / corridor geometry.

That would make FLOOR destructive beyond the clicked cell and could become
very surprising.

Keep the concepts distinct:

    FLOOR = cell contents
    ERASE = explicit destructive editing tool
    THIN = edge geometry

==================================================
8. OPTIONAL FLOOR CONVENIENCE
==================================================

If desired, FLOOR may clear edge geometry ONLY when the click itself is in the
edge hit-zone.

But a centre-cell FLOOR click should not wipe all neighbouring edges.

The explicit ERASE mode should be the canonical way to remove arbitrary
geometry.

==================================================
9. MAKE EDGE TARGETING LESS FIDDLY
==================================================

Current EDGE_HIT is approximately 4px in a 16px cell.

When:

    THIN mode
or
    ERASE mode

is active, widen the edge hit region because the user's intent is specifically
to interact with boundaries.

For example, increase the hit tolerance enough that selecting an edge feels
deliberate but does not make corners ambiguous.

If a click falls near a corner where both H and V are candidates, choose the
nearest edge geometrically rather than toggling both.

==================================================
10. ERASE VISUAL FEEDBACK
==================================================

When hovering in ERASE mode, highlight exactly what will be deleted:

    cell
or
    horizontal edge
or
    vertical edge
or
    decoration

This is much better than forcing the user to guess which 4px hit zone they are
inside.

The existing problem is partly discoverability, not only data mutation.

==================================================
11. KEEP THIN EDGE TOGGLE AS A SHORTCUT
==================================================

The existing THIN behaviour:

    click empty edge -> create
    click occupied edge -> remove

can stay.

It is a useful quick toggle.

But ERASE provides an unambiguous second route for removing an edge.

Do not remove the existing working toggle merely because ERASE exists.

